{"name":"Benthic Black","hex":"#000011","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"benthic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to the benthos; living on the seafloor, as opposed to floating in the ocean."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any organism that lives on the seafloor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/benthic"},{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"}],"entries":[{"title":"Benthic zone","blocks":[{"text":"The benthic zone, from Ancient Greek  βένθος (bénthos) 'the depths of the ocean', is the ecological region at the lowest level of a body of water, such as a stream, river, lake, or ocean, including the sediment surface and some sub-surface layers."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benthic_zone","wikidata":"Q686881"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcse